


NEWFS(8)	    UNIX Programmer's Manual		 NEWFS(8)



NNAAMMEE
     newfs - construct a new file system

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     //eettcc//nneewwffss [ --NN ] [ --bb ] [ --BB bboooottbblloocckk ] ssppeecciiaall ddiisskk--ttyyppee
     ccppuu--ttyyppee

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     _N_e_w_f_s is a ``friendly'' front-end to the _m_k_f_s(8) program.
     _N_e_w_f_s will look up the type of disk a file system is being
     created on in the disk description file /_e_t_c/_d_i_s_k_t_a_b, calcu-
     late the appropriate parameters to use in calling _m_k_f_s, then
     build the file system by forking _m_k_f_s.  The -_N option causes
     the file system parameters to be printed out without actu-
     ally creating the file system.

     The -_band-_B options specify that a primary block zero
     bootstrap should be installed in block zero of _s_p_e_c_i_a_l.
     This is typically done for `a' partitions to make disks
     bootable.	If -_b is specified, _n_e_w_f_s will attempt to use a
     default boot block (defined in /_e_t_c/_d_i_s_k_t_a_b).  The -_B option
     allows one to specify the file to read the boot block from
     (_b_o_o_t_b_l_o_c_k).  Note that a boot block can always be copied
     separately via:

	  dddd iiff==_b_o_o_t_b_l_o_c_k ooff==_s_p_e_c_i_a_l bbss==551122 ccoouunntt==11 ccoonnvv==ssyynncc

     _N_e_w_f_s without arguments will print a list of the disk and
     cpu types that it is familiar with.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/disktab   for disk geometry and file system partition
     information
     /etc/mkfs to actually build the file system
     /mdec/*   block zero bootstraps

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     disktab(5), fs(5), diskpart(8), fsck(8), format(8), mkfs(8),
     tunefs(8)

BBUUGGSS
     Should figure out the disk and cpu types without the user's
     help.

     The standard 4.3BSD C libarary disktab structure, as
     declared in /_u_s_r/_i_n_c_l_u_d_e/_d_i_s_k_t_a_b._h, was not sufficient to
     convey the information the PDP-11 needs, such as the rela-
     tionship between the cpu type and the M/N values.	There-
     fore, the relevant values were extracted from the
     /_e_t_c/_d_i_s_k_t_a_b file and hardwired into the _n_e_w_f_s program.  If
     you have an unknown disk or cpu type, you may want to read
     through /_e_t_c/_d_i_s_k_t_a_b and _n_e_w_f_s to find a similar disk or



